hey everyone this is kevin from  thechesswebsite.com and today we're  going to be going over a trap in the  italian game it's the chagoran trap and  this was played from an actual game from  shigorn and so that's how it got the  name but the italian  game is one of the most popular openings  in all chess starts out with e4 e5  knight f3 knight c6 bishop to c4 and  then we're going to be looking at the  gioco piano with bishop c5 now white has  lots of ways to respond but decides to  go ahead and play the castle on at the  king sideline knight to f6 and then  after d3  d6 this is where we start getting into  the trap  white plays bishop g5  and then black pushes forward with h6  forcing the bishop to come back many  times if you play bishop g5 pinning down  the knights you want to maintain  pressure with your bishop and you bring  your bishop back to h4 now you're okay  if your opponent plays g5 because  although you can no longer maintain the  pressure on this pin  your opponent has now extended the pawns  farther than they would want to on the  king side of the board so bishop comes  back g3 and this is where we lay our  trap we play the move pawn h5 and it  looks pretty weird because the pawn on  g5 no longer has protection and even  from white standpoint if they think  there's some trap well okay the the  queen isn't really involved into the  game and it can get involved into the  game because it's gonna have to move  past this knight on f6  it can't come up to d7 or get involved  without a few moves here and so from  white's standpoint they can take on g5  and have the immediate threat of white  takes on f7  attacking both the rook and the queen  being protected by the bishop it seems  very difficult for black to have a  threat but that's exactly  the trap we are going to lay on our  opponent and that's the same trap that  shogorn laid when he played this trap as  black play continues with pawn to h4 and  you can start to see the threat that  black has attacking the bishop the  bishop cannot move anywhere it is now  completely trapped but that's not the  only threat that black has in store  now because the bishop is completely  lost  it essentially is coming down to how can  white now  maximize the amount of material they're  going to get and the right move for  white is to play knight takes on f7 yes  they could also take with the bishop on  f7  but this just isn't as ideal because  king 2 e7 and then you're just going to  see some exchanges yes remember the pawn  is attacking the bishop so the bishop  can decide let's just go and start  taking material off the board knight  takes on e5 maybe d4 opening up the  center uh now attacking both these minor  pieces can just take that bishop off on  f7 and after some of the exchanges here  uh you you can see that  yes the king is exposed here on f7  but the queen on d1 although it starts  to attack it's going to be difficult to  attack all alone and with none of the  bishops on the side for white  black's in a pretty good spot here so if  we come back instead of the bishop  taking on f7 it makes a lot more sense  to go ahead and continue with the same  game plan of knight 2 f7 and now they're  attacking the queen  on d8 and also the rook on h8 which is  currently protecting the pawn on h4  and you can start to see what black is  thinking about because the queen on d8  remember when you're playing these traps  it's not about how much material you  have on the board it's what material you  have involved in the attack so we can  see and we already pointed out the queen  on d8 is not really involved and it's  going to take a few moves to get  involved so we don't really care about  it we're going to sacrifice that queen  we're first going to take with our pawn  on g3 and take that bishop  so white's going to continue take the  queen on d8 and now we're going to play  bishop g4 now we're attacking the queen  you can see we also have the threat of  the bishop on c5 that's pinning down  this pawn so the pawn just can't take  here on g3 that would be an illegal move  and so we start to have a lot of threats  here now the the queen's gonna be forced  to move here because since the  the pawn can't move no longer has the  knight to block it and the bishop  can't come over here because it's  blocked by its own pawn chain here so  queen to d2 makes a lot of sense you  still want to maintain pressure on the  f2 square since black is attacking it  and something like e1 this now blocks  off the rook from ever coming uh to e1  d1 or  c1 so probably the best move is just to  play  queen to  d2  but from here black plays knight d4 and  it has a lot of threats one of the  threats is to play the move  knight to e2 this is check and so you  know attacking the the king here is one  of the immediate threats in the  particular game and we're going to come  back to a few situations and see how  white could have responded differently  but one of the threats that we're  looking at  is the night coming to e2 and so in the  actual game that shkorn played  white played the move knight to c3 and  said i see this knight to e2 move and so  i'm gonna stop that with my knight on c3  the problem is this is actually just  losing for  white  and this shows the power of the trap  it's really difficult to see all the  threats until it plays out because black  has the move knights f3 we already  talked about that the pawn can't take on  a g3 and points being pinned down now  this is check if the king just comes to  h1 we can see that's going to be  checkmate with our rook on h2 so that is  not an option the only other option is  to take the knight on f3 but once white  does that black can take with the bishop  on f3 and there's no way to really stop  the mating pattern let's say they take  with the pawn on  uh g3 okay we'll now just rook to h1  that is going to be checkmate  if they  don't move uh that the pawn to g3 maybe  they try to stop it uh they just play  pawn to h3  okay well now we're just going to play  rook to h3  that's not going to work as well maybe  they decide to go ahead and  free up their king so they move their  rook to  c1 and now we play pawn takes on h2  king 2f1 and now queen h1  so you can see that white really doesn't  have a  spot to move in black's next move is  just to threaten with pawn takes on h2  and this is also going to be checkmate  the pawn is protected by the rook and  the square here on h1 and g2 are both  protected by the bishop on f3 so you can  see there's just no way around it in the  actual game uh white did take here on g3  and then rook h1 this was checkmate so  if we come back we can see that knight  to c3 is not going to stop the threat  now what if white plays  h3 okay well this makes some sense uh  but from here black can play the move  knight to e to check  now let's look at a few options of what  this would  end up if they look at and say okay that  the king needs to move it can't come to  h2 if it comes to h2 or excuse me h1  this is going to end up in checkmate  rook takes on h3 the pawn takes and now  bishop f3  this is checkmate the only option that  white has  is to actually take the queen  remember black gave up the queen on d8  so it's not terrible for white to give  back their queen but this is just losing  for white the bishop takes on e2  and potentially now they play knight to  e6  they would do this because they re  recognized that okay now it comes down  to there's no mating threats going on  anymore and so how can they maintain as  much of their material as possible their  knight is under attack their rook is  also under attack on f1 so they have to  keep that in mind but the knight here on  e6  does want to make sure that they don't  just lose this and it's also attacking  the bishop on c5 best move for black is  to play bishop to b6 the reason for that  is black really wants to maintain this  pressure on this long dark diagonal  because we want to give white more  opportunities  to make a mistake one of those mistakes  if they look at and say okay my knight  is safe now  but now if i look at my rook  i need to move that rook  and so if they play rook to e1 this  would be a massive mistake because yes  the rook's not going to be taken by the  bishop but now they're just going to end  up losing the game starting with pawn  takes on f2 check  you can see if the king comes to h1 then  just the pawn takes on  uh e1 gets a queen and this is going to  be uh checkmate the pawn can't take it's  being pinned down by the rook if instead  the king doesn't come to h1  now maybe we have king to h2 okay well  it's going to be same thing  knight to g4 check  maybe even king 2 g3 but then we can  take on e1 we can promote to a queen or  a bishop i think it's funnier if we take  with our pawn and promote to a bishop  just because this is a pretty  interesting way to checkmate but all in  all it does not matter  if they decide  hey i want to hold on to this rook and  move that rook they are going to lose so  if we look at this after bishop to b6  later we can take their rook on f1 we're  going to get some of that material back  if you look at and say well lights up a  couple  points from the pawn standpoint yes we  are now going to take their rook we can  also take their pawn put pressure on uh  this f2 square since we are protected  with our bishop so definitely going to  be  an interesting in game but but i think  black has the advantage in this spot so  hopefully you guys enjoyed this trap  always fun to see these older games with  some of the top players they were very  creative in their traps and how that can  apply uh and we can start to see all the  threats that they were looking at and  one of the most popular openings in the  italian game so thank you so much for  watching hopefully you enjoyed it if you  have other ideas on videos let me know  in the comments below but i'll see you  guys in the next video

